---
description: Comment style and push-approval workflow for this repo
alwaysApply: true
---

# No internal tracking references in code comments

Never write comments like `// item 16, Problem 3` or `// see pending_items.md` or
`// TR-033` or any other reference to this project's internal planning docs,
issue numbers, or tracking IDs. Nobody reading this code from GitHub has that
context, and `plans/` is gitignored anyway (not visible to them at all).

- Bad: `// item 16, Problem 3 fencing token -- see heartbeat.py's docstring`
- Good: `// Fencing token: bumped on every reclaim so a runner that gets superseded
  // mid-execution (network blip, then finishes anyway) can be told to stop
  // instead of clobbering the runner that replaced it.`

Every comment must be self-contained: explain the *why* directly, as if the
reader has never seen any planning doc, review thread, or issue tracker. If you
catch yourself typing "item ", "Problem ", "TR-0", "IR-0", "REL-0", "SEC-0",
"DEP-0", "FUN-0", "API-0", "CH-0", or "master plan" inside a code comment, stop
and rewrite it generically. "(master plan: "...")" is the same class of
violation as "item 16" -- it's a citation to an internal planning doc
(`plans/master_plan.md`, gitignored, never pushed) that a GitHub reader has no
access to, even though the quoted text after it is often genuinely useful
context. Keep that context, drop the citation: fold the quoted description
directly into the sentence instead of framing it as a reference to something
external.

- Bad: `// Package tenant implements multi-tenancy (master plan: "workspace/org/team
  // hierarchy with isolated data") as a flat tenant_id scope...`
- Good: `// Package tenant implements multi-tenancy -- a flat tenant_id scope
  // (not a full workspace/org/team hierarchy) threaded through...`

This applies to source comments in Go/Python/TypeScript/Lua and to test
names/docstrings -- internal doc structure can change; the code comment must
still make sense on its own five years from now.

# Never push without explicit approval

Never run `git push` (or open a PR) unless the user explicitly says so in that
message. Committing locally is fine and expected as work completes. This holds
across every session in this repo -- don't wait to be told again.
